\section{Adaptation Motivation and Sea-State Scaling}
\label{sec:adaptation-motivation}
\label{app:rs-scaling}

The core estimator and the regularization analysis above are independent of how
bounded model parameters are scheduled.  This section asks the engineering
question that follows from them: how should a practical marine implementation
move the OU time scale, stationary acceleration scale, and integral-state
regularization as the wave regime changes?  The purpose is to identify sensible
sea-state coordinates and build a candidate schedule.  The final coefficients
and amplitude dependence are selected and tested with the complete estimator;
this section does not claim a closed-form optimum for the 21-state MEKF.

The starting point is the third-integral coordinate itself.  For a scalar
acceleration process with characteristic standard deviation $\sigma_{aw}$ and
time scale $\tau$, dimensional similarity gives the natural state scales
\[
a_w\sim\sigma_{aw},\qquad
v\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau,\qquad
p\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau^2,\qquad
S\sim\sigma_{aw}\tau^3.
\]
The result is made exact for the OU prior below.  Because an unregularized third
integral is not stationary, the theorem is a similarity statement; it does not
by itself determine the pseudo-measurement covariance or the full-filter
regularization strength.

